#!/usr/bin/env bash
# docker — a thin serialization shim in FRONT of the real docker CLI, for the
# ci-runner agent ONLY (runner@.service puts this directory first on PATH).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# All CI runner slots share ONE rootless podman instance (one storage under
# /home/ci-runner/.local/share/containers, one API socket). `podman network prune`
# is NOT concurrency-safe against container removal: to decide which networks are
# still in use it walks the GLOBAL container database — the `--filter label=...`
# only narrows what it DELETES, not what it SCANS. So when job A removes its
# container while job B prunes its own network, B's scan trips over the container
# A just deleted and the whole prune fails:
#
#     [command]/usr/bin/docker rm --force fe249311…      -> fe249311…   (A: succeeds)
#     [command]/usr/bin/docker network prune --force --filter label=<B's label>
#     Error response from daemon: no container with ID 3724a5df… found in database
#                                                       ^^^^^^^^ that is A's container
#
# The job's own work has already passed at that point; only the runner's "Stop
# containers" teardown fails — but it still reds the job. It needs real
# concurrency to appear, so a single-slot pool never saw it. Observed live
# 2026-07-14 on a 12-job matrix: 12/12 green once, then 10/12 and 8/12 RED.
#
# THE FIX
# -------
# Nothing may mutate podman's global container/network database while a prune is
# scanning it. A readers-writer lock expresses exactly that, and nothing more:
#
#   * `network prune`                      -> EXCLUSIVE (-x): no mutation may overlap it.
#   * `create` / `rm` / other `network` ops -> SHARED    (-s): they may run concurrently
#                                             with each other (they never raced), but
#                                             never during a prune.
#   * everything else (pull, ps, start, exec, inspect, logs, …) -> UNLOCKED.
#
# So the SLOW operations — image pull, container start, and every `docker exec`
# that runs the actual job steps — stay fully parallel across slots. Only the
# sub-second prune is serialized against removals, so the parallelism this whole
# runner pool exists for is preserved.
#
# This is a workaround for a podman-side race, held here (rather than by
# serializing the container hooks wholesale) because it is the narrowest place
# that fixes it: the hooks would serialize container SETUP and TEARDOWN entirely,
# putting a multi-minute serial floor under a 60-job matrix.
set -uo pipefail

# THE CONTAINER HOOKS INVOKE US WITH A SCRUBBED ENVIRONMENT, and rootless podman
# cannot run without the three variables they drop. The runner's hook layer hands
# the docker CLI the JOB CONTAINER's environment rather than the runner account's,
# so what arrives is (observed live 2026-08-13, by dumping `env` from this shim on
# a `start` call) only HOME and DOCKER_HOST — HOME pointing INSIDE the container:
#
#   HOME=/github/home
#   DOCKER_HOST=unix:///run/user/1001/podman/podman.sock
#
# Docker-the-CLI tolerates that. podman does not, because it derives real paths
# from all three:
#
#   HOME             podman resolves its storage/config roots from $HOME, so the
#                    container's /github/home (which does not exist on the host)
#                    kills `docker create` before it does any work:
#                      cannot resolve /github/home: lstat /github: no such file
#                    That is the FIRST failure of a containerized job. What the
#                    runner then surfaces is a downstream red herring — the next
#                    step reads a null container and reports
#                      TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'container')
#                    so the visible error names the JS hook layer, not this.
#   PATH             absent entirely; podman looks up its network helper on PATH:
#                      setting up Pasta: could not find pasta ... in $PATH
#   XDG_RUNTIME_DIR  absent; podman falls off the per-user runtime dir holding the
#                    rootless socket and the lock state LOCK below is built from.
#
# Restore all three from the INVOKING ACCOUNT rather than trusting what was
# inherited. This is the same docker-vs-podman compat seam the bare-`logout`
# translation below occupies: the hooks are not wrong by docker's contract, so the
# translation belongs here rather than in the hooks.
#
# Replaying a failing `docker create` BY HAND always succeeded, which is what made
# this look like a payload or version problem — a hand shell simply has the real
# environment. Bisect by environment, not by argv.
#
# CAPTURE BEFORE OVERRIDING: `docker create` carries a BARE `-e HOME` (no
# `=value`) — docker's "pass through MY OWN HOME into the container" convention —
# and it is BAKED into the container's persistent env at create time, becoming
# the default HOME for every later `exec` that does not specify its own. If we
# export the repaired (real-host) HOME first and let that flag pick it up, the
# container's baked HOME becomes the HOST account's home (e.g. /home/ci-runner)
# instead of whatever the hooks intended (typically /github/home) — invisible
# until a later exec step, inside that same container, does something HOME-
# sensitive. Observed live 2026-08-13: `git config --global --add
# safe.directory` failed with `could not lock config file
# /home/ci-runner/.gitconfig: No such file or directory` — the HOST path,
# leaked into a container session, on a path that does not exist there.
# Preserve the ORIGINAL value so a bare `-e HOME` still bakes what it always
# baked; only THIS shim's OWN process (talking to the podman socket) gets the
# repaired one.
_ORIGINAL_HOME="${HOME-}"
HOME="$(getent passwd "$(id -u)" | cut -d: -f6)"
export HOME
# Append rather than replace: a PATH the hooks DID set stays authoritative, and
# the system directories podman needs are guaranteed present either way. Safe to
# export unconditionally — unlike HOME, the real `create` argv never bare-passes
# PATH or XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (verified against the live command 2026-08-13), so
# there is no create-time bake-in path for either to leak into a container.
export PATH="${PATH:+$PATH:}/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"
export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}"

# Rewrite a bare `-e HOME` / `--env HOME` (no `=value`) back to the ORIGINAL
# HOME, so the container is baked with what the hooks intended rather than this
# shim's own repaired HOME. Populates the global array REWRITTEN_ARGV; only
# rewrites when the original HOME was actually set — an unset original leaves
# the flag as-is, matching docker's own bare-passthrough behavior for an unset
# variable. Bash array (not a printf/newline pipeline) so an argument
# containing whitespace round-trips unchanged.
_rewrite_home_passthrough() {
  REWRITTEN_ARGV=()
  local skip_next=0
  for arg in "$@"; do
    if [ "$skip_next" = 1 ]; then
      skip_next=0
      if [ "$arg" = "HOME" ] && [ -n "$_ORIGINAL_HOME" ]; then
        REWRITTEN_ARGV+=("HOME=$_ORIGINAL_HOME")
      else
        REWRITTEN_ARGV+=("$arg")
      fi
      continue
    fi
    case "$arg" in
      -e | --env)
        skip_next=1
        REWRITTEN_ARGV+=("$arg")
        ;;
      *) REWRITTEN_ARGV+=("$arg") ;;
    esac
  done
}

# Both overridable so dockershim-exit-tests.sh can drive the lock discipline
# against a fake docker without a podman on the box. Defaults are the live paths.
REAL_DOCKER="${CI_RUNNER_REAL_DOCKER:-/usr/bin/docker}"
# ci-runner-owned tmpfs; flock creates the file if absent.
LOCK="${CI_RUNNER_PODMAN_LOCK:-${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/1001}/ci-runner-podman-db.lock}"

# THE SUBCOMMAND IS NOT ALWAYS $1. The container hooks invoke docker with GLOBAL
# options before the subcommand — e.g.
#   docker --config /…/_temp/.docker_<uuid> login -u … --password-stdin ghcr.io
#   docker --config /…/_temp/.docker_<uuid> logout
# so reading $1 as the subcommand yields "--config" and every lock decision below
# is silently wrong for exactly the calls the hooks make. Scan past leading global
# options instead, consuming the value of the ones that take one.
_docker_subcommand() {
  local skip_next=0
  for arg in "$@"; do
    if [ "$skip_next" = 1 ]; then skip_next=0; continue; fi
    case "$arg" in
      # Global options that take a separate value.
      --config | --context | -c | --host | -H | --log-level | -l \
      | --tlscacert | --tlscert | --tlskey)
        skip_next=1
        ;;
      # Any other option (boolean, or --opt=value) carries no separate value.
      -*) ;;
      *) printf '%s\n' "$arg"; return 0 ;;
    esac
  done
  printf '\n'
}

sub="$(_docker_subcommand "$@")"

# Apply the HOME-passthrough rewrite ONLY for `create` — that is the ONE
# subcommand whose real argv carries a bare `-e HOME`, confirmed by reading the
# live command (2026-08-13); `start`, `exec`, `rm`, `network`, `login`, `pull`
# never do. `set --` replaces the positional parameters so every dispatch below
# — the lock-wrapped exec, the bind-source loop, the final passthrough — sees
# the rewritten argv without each call site needing its own rewrite.
if [ "$sub" = "create" ]; then
  _rewrite_home_passthrough "$@"
  set -- "${REWRITTEN_ARGV[@]}"
fi

# Positional arguments AFTER the subcommand, used only to distinguish
# `network prune` from other network verbs and to detect a bare `logout`.
_docker_operands() {
  local seen_sub=0 skip_next=0
  for arg in "$@"; do
    if [ "$skip_next" = 1 ]; then skip_next=0; continue; fi
    case "$arg" in
      --config | --context | -c | --host | -H | --log-level | -l \
      | --tlscacert | --tlscert | --tlskey)
        [ "$seen_sub" = 1 ] && printf '%s\n' "$arg"
        skip_next=1
        ;;
      -*) ;;
      *)
        if [ "$seen_sub" = 1 ]; then printf '%s\n' "$arg"; else seen_sub=1; fi
        ;;
    esac
  done
}

operand1="$(_docker_operands "$@" | head -1)"

# DOCKER-VS-PODMAN COMPAT: a bare `logout`. Docker logs out of its default
# registry when given none; podman REFUSES, and the container hooks always call
# it bare during teardown:
#   Error: no registries found in registries.conf, a registry must be provided
# That exits 125 and reds the job AFTER its work has already passed — the network
# was created, the ghcr.io login succeeded, and the image pulled. `--all` is the
# faithful translation: log out of everything this job logged into. Observed
# 2026-08-13 on a fresh host; it is the last thing standing between a correctly
# provisioned host and a green containerized job.
if [ "$sub" = "logout" ] && [ -z "$operand1" ]; then
  exec "$REAL_DOCKER" "$@" --all
fi

# DOCKER-VS-PODMAN COMPAT: missing bind-mount SOURCE directories. Docker creates
# a bind source that does not exist; podman refuses and exits 125:
#   Error: statfs /home/ci-runner/runners/<slot>/_work/_actions: no such file or
#   directory
# The runner emits `_work/_actions`, `_work/_tool` and `_work/_temp/_github_*` as
# system mounts BEFORE it has created them — on a fresh slot they appear first at
# container-prepare time — so every containerized job on a slot that has not run
# one before dies here. Observed live 2026-08-13 on slot 11, as the NEXT failure
# after the scrubbed-environment fix above stopped masking it.
#
# Create them, which is exactly what dockerd would have done. Scoped narrowly:
# only on `create`, only for absolute paths (a leading `/` is what distinguishes
# a bind from a NAMED volume, which podman manages itself and which must not be
# turned into a stray directory), and the container-side half of `SRC:DST` is
# never touched. A path that already exists is left alone, so permissions and
# ownership of a warm cache are never rewritten.
if [ "$sub" = "create" ]; then
  for _arg in "$@"; do
    case "$_arg" in
      -v=/*|--volume=/*) _spec="${_arg#*=}" ;;
      /*:/*)             _spec="$_arg" ;;
      *)                 continue ;;
    esac
    _src="${_spec%%:*}"
    [ -n "$_src" ] && [ ! -e "$_src" ] && mkdir -p "$_src" 2>/dev/null
  done
fi

if [ "$sub" = "network" ] && [ "$operand1" = "prune" ]; then
  exec /usr/bin/flock -x "$LOCK" "$REAL_DOCKER" "$@"
fi

# DOCKER-VS-PODMAN COMPAT: podman cannot tear down its own rootless network
# helper from the API service, and reds an already-passed job for it:
#   Error: cleaning up container <id>: removing container <id> network:
#     * rootless netns: kill network process: permission denied
#   exit 125
# podman does the teardown from inside its rootless USER NAMESPACE while pasta
# runs outside it (reparented to PID 1), and signalling across that boundary is
# EPERM. It is NOT a plain permission problem — `kill -0` on that same pasta pid
# from a ci-runner shell succeeds — and NOT a concurrency race: it reproduces
# with ref-count 1 and a single container, and with this shim bypassed entirely.
#
# The container itself IS removed; only the helper cleanup fails. So the failure
# is reported on work that already succeeded, exactly like the bare-`logout`
# case above.
#
# Tolerate it, but NEVER blindly: swallow the failure only when the error is
# THIS one AND every container named on the command line is genuinely gone. Any
# other error, or a container still present, exits with podman's own status. The
# original output is always reproduced on stderr, so nothing is hidden — only
# the exit status is translated.
if [ "$sub" = "rm" ]; then
  _out="$(/usr/bin/flock -s "$LOCK" "$REAL_DOCKER" "$@" 2>&1)"
  _status=$?
  if [ "$_status" -eq 0 ]; then
    [ -n "$_out" ] && printf '%s\n' "$_out"
    exit 0
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$_out" >&2
  case "$_out" in
    *"rootless netns"*"kill network process"*) ;;
    *) exit "$_status" ;;
  esac
  _still_present=0
  _operands="$(_docker_operands "$@")"
  for _id in $_operands; do
    if "$REAL_DOCKER" inspect "$_id" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      _still_present=1
    fi
  done
  if [ "$_still_present" -eq 1 ]; then
    printf 'dockershim: rootless-netns teardown failed AND a container survives; not tolerated\n' >&2
    exit "$_status"
  fi
  printf 'dockershim: tolerated podman rootless-netns teardown failure (containers removed)\n' >&2
  exit 0
fi

case "$sub" in
  create | network)
    exec /usr/bin/flock -s "$LOCK" "$REAL_DOCKER" "$@"
    ;;
esac

# DOCKER-VS-PODMAN COMPAT: a masked upstream podman bug corrupts `exec` under
# concurrency. libpod's SQLiteState.UpdateContainer (podman-container-tools/
# podman, libpod/sqlite_state.go) only returns early when its container-state
# row Scan fails with sql.ErrNoRows; any OTHER scan error (confirmed present
# in podman main as of 2026-08-13, unfixed upstream) falls through and
# unmarshals an EMPTY string, producing:
#   Error: syncing container <id> state to update exec session <id>:
#   unmarshalling container state JSON: readObjectStart: expect { or n,
#   but found  , error found in #0 byte of ...
# Observed live under this pool's concurrency (2/71 jobs in one self-hosted
# run, 0/75 in the very next) — see plan/fleet-ci-runner-pool/handoff.md
# (livespec repo) "Issue C". This is a transient scan failure inside podman,
# not a real exec failure, so a bounded retry recovers it without masking a
# genuine command failure: only THIS exact signature retries, everything
# else forwards immediately on its first attempt.
#
# Deliberately UNLOCKED, unlike `create`/`network` above — see the header.
# `exec` is the highest-volume, most latency-sensitive operation in the
# whole shim (every job step runs one), so this wraps it in a bounded retry
# rather than a lock: a lock here would serialize the very parallelism this
# pool exists for, and would not even address this bug — the corrupting
# read/write both happen INSIDE podman's own SQLite layer, a resource this
# shim's flock has no visibility into.
#
# Raised from 3 to 10 attempts when per-repo slot counts moved from a shared
# ~50-concurrent ceiling to each repo's own full CI-matrix width (up to 96) —
# per-run exec volume against the shared SQLite state scales with matrix
# width times steps-per-job, not with slot count alone, so a wider matrix
# means more independent chances to collide even though the collision rate
# per exec call is unchanged. Still bounded and still scoped to the exact
# error signature: a genuinely different exec failure still forwards on its
# first attempt.
if [ "$sub" = "exec" ]; then
  _errfile="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/ci-runner-dockershim-exec.XXXXXX")"
  _attempt=1
  while :; do
    "$REAL_DOCKER" "$@" 2>"$_errfile"
    _status=$?
    [ "$_status" -eq 0 ] && break
    if [ "$_attempt" -ge 10 ] \
      || ! grep -q 'unmarshalling container.*state JSON' "$_errfile" \
      || ! grep -q 'readObjectStart: expect { or n' "$_errfile"; then
      break
    fi
    _attempt=$((_attempt + 1))
    sleep 1
  done
  cat "$_errfile" >&2
  rm -f "$_errfile"
  exit "$_status"
fi

exec "$REAL_DOCKER" "$@"
